[Theorem thm:hl of arXiv:2601.04997, 'Fluctuations of the Boundary-Driven Symmetric Zero-Range Process from the NESS'] Let $\gamma:[0,1]\to[0, \infty)$ be a  measurable function and let $\{\mu^N\}_N$ be a sequence of probability measures on $\Omega_N$ associated to $\gamma$ and satisfying the two conditions 
\begin{equation*}
    H(\mu^N|\bar\nu_N)\lesssim N \quad \text{and} \quad \mu^N\le \bar\nu_N.
\end{equation*}
Then, for $\theta\ge1$ and for every $t\in [0,T]$, the sequence of empirical measures $\{\pi^{N}_t\}_N$ converges in probability to the absolutely continuous measure $\rho(t, u)\de u$, that is, for any $\varepsilon>0$ and for any $H\in C^2(\mathbb{T})$, we have that
\begin{equation*}
    \mylim_{N\to\infty}\mathbb{P}_{\mu^N} \left\{\left|\langle \pi^{N}_{t},H\rangle-\int_0^1\rho(t,u)H(u)\de u \right|>\varepsilon\right\} =0,
\end{equation*}
where $\rho(t,u)$ is the unique weak solution of (equation eq:pde_zr of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

