{"artifact_digest":"sha256:ab036f8c1cc14ccfafd4cc35eb73aa3f649052c3cf975980472eeaab6193a241","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/ab/ab036f8c1cc14ccfafd4cc35eb73aa3f649052c3cf975980472eeaab6193a241.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:635ffbef43fd60417a401202d20eea47061de27882d31a41891e2d616f66e4f6","bundle_entry_key":"2412-04396-theorem-6-proposition-6","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s1_120","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s1_120.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2412-04396-proposition-6-proposition-6","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: To prove the tightness of $\pi^n$, it is enough to prove the tightness of $\langle \pi^n, G\rangle $ for any smooth function $G:{\mathbb T} \to {\mathbb R}$.  Consider the Dynkin's martingale
	\begin{equation}
		M_t^n(G)\;=\;  \langle \pi^n_t, G\rangle-\langle \pi^n_0, G\rangle - k^2 n^{1+\beta}\int_0^t {\mathcal L}_n \langle \pi^n_s, G\rangle ds\,.
	\end{equation}
	A straightforward computation gives that
	\begin{equation}
