body:
[Theorem of arXiv:2608.10415, 'Continuous random variables'] When flipping a biased coin $n$ times in a row:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $E({\rm heads})=np$.

\item $E({\rm tails})=n(1-p)$.

\item $E(a\times{\rm heads}+b\times{\rm tails})=n(ap+b(1-p))$.
\end{enumerate}

[Thm thm-cell-ldp of arXiv:2510.26123, 'Directed distances in bipolar-oriented triangulations:\\ exact exponents and scaling limit'] For each $\delta \in (0,1)$, there exists $A = A(\delta) >1$ such that for each $n\in\mathbbm N$, it holds with probability at least $1-\delta$ that 
\begin{equation}  
 A^{-1} n^{3/4} \leq \sup\mathopen{}\mathclose\bgroup\originalleft\{ \operatorname{LDP}_{M_{0,n}}(x,y)  : x,y\in\mathcal V(M_{0,n}) \aftergroup\egroup\originalright\} \leq A n^{3/4} ,   
\end{equation} 
where here we recall that $\operatorname{LDP}_{M_{0,n}}(x,y) = -\infty$ if there is no directed path in $M_{0,n}$ from $x$ to $y$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

