{"artifact_digest":"sha256:1099e5540616c5481f6189a37a371490b55640320c74037d1741899dbcb7367d","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/10/1099e5540616c5481f6189a37a371490b55640320c74037d1741899dbcb7367d.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:ac48c77da68e80067d248464f87e25b5b04d0ad38afd1011f1ba95e119c86645","bundle_entry_key":"2608-09105-lemma-23-concatenation","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s0_114","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s0_114.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2608-09105-lemma-23-concatenation","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: From (equation triangle_inclusion of the paper) we know
\eq{
\mathrm{Interior}(\mathsf{Tri}_f(a,x)) \subseteq \mathrm{Interior}(\mathsf{Tri}_f(a,b)) \quad \text{and} \quad
\mathrm{Interior}(\mathsf{Tri}_f(x,b)) \subseteq \mathrm{Interior}(\mathsf{Tri}_f(a,b)).
}
This ensures $\mathcal{C}_1\cup\mathcal{C}_2\subset\mathrm{Interior}(\mathsf{Tri}_f(a,b))$.

Now we argue that $\{\mathsf{A},\mathsf{B}\}\cup\mathcal{C}_1\cup\mathcal{C}_2$ is in convex position.
There is nothing to show if $\mathcal{C}_1$ and $\mathcal{C}_2$ are both empty.
So we assume $\mathcal{C}_1$ is nonempty; the argument is similar if $\mathcal{C}_2$ is nonempty.
