[Proposition 6 of arXiv:2608.30570, '2608.30570'] (\cite{HL2})
		On a 2 dimensional translating soliton $\Sigma$ in ${\mathbb R}^4$, the following identity holds:


		\begin{equation}
			\Delta\langle {\bf T},x\rangle=\Delta x_1=|{\bf H}|^2,
		\end{equation}
		where $x$ is the position vector in ${\mathbb R}^4$; for any $J\in {\mathcal J}$,
		\begin{equation}
			\Delta\cos\alpha_J+\langle\nabla x_1,\nabla\cos\alpha_J\rangle=-|\overline{\nabla}J_{\Sigma}|^2\cos\alpha_J,
		\end{equation}
		where $|\overline{\nabla}J_{\Sigma}|^2=|h^1_{2i}+h^2_{1i}|^2+|h^1_{1i}-h^2_{2i}|^2$ which is independent of the choice of oriented orthonormal frame;

		\begin{equation}
			\Delta|{\bf H}|^2+\langle\nabla x_1,\nabla|{\bf H}|^2\rangle\geq2|\nabla{\bf H}|^2-2|{\bf A}|^2|{\bf H}|^2;
		\end{equation}

        		\begin{equation}
			\Delta|{\bf A}|^2+\langle\nabla x_1,\nabla|{\bf A}|^2\rangle\geq2|\nabla{\bf H}|^2-3|{\bf A}|^4.
		\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

