[Corollary cor:conditional-collision-phase of arXiv:2609.01690, 'Iterated-sumset spectra: The complete exponent law and its rank geometry'] Fix $k\geq3$.  There is a unique number
$\theta_k\in(0,1/3)$ satisfying
\begin{equation}
 (1-\theta_k)^{k-1}
 -3(1-2\theta_k)^{k-1}
 +2(1-3\theta_k)^{k-1}=0.
\end{equation}
If $A_q$ is uniform in $\binom{[q]}k$, then, with the limits taken in
the displayed order,
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{\quad
 \lim_{h\to\infty}\lim_{q\to\infty}
 \mathbb P\left(
  C_2(A_q;h)>C_{\geq3}(A_q;h)
  \mathrel{\Big|} A_q\text{ is not }B_h
 \right)
 =1-\theta_k^{\,k-2}.
 \quad}
\end{equation}
For $k=4$,
\[
 \theta_4=\frac{21-\sqrt{69}}{62},
 \qquad
 1-\theta_4^2=0.9580848601\ldots.
\]

