[Corollary cor:exp_mom_local_time of arXiv:2512.17747, 'On exponentially height-penalized random trees'] We denote by $B^{(n)}_{x,y}$ a uniform bridge of length $n$ from $x$ to $y$, that is a uniform element from $\mathcal B^{(n)}_{x,y}$.
    Then, for any $s\in\mathbb R$ and $A>0$, there exists $M>0$ such that, for all $n\geq1$ and all $0\leq x,y\leq A\sqrt{n}$, we have
    \begin{align*}
        \Exp{\exp\left(s\frac{w\big(B^{(n)}_{x,y}\big)}{\sqrt{n}}\right)}\leq M\,.
    \end{align*}

