[Proposition Theorem~2.3 in \citet{hult2006regular} of arXiv:2605.10491, 'Zero-couplings of infinite measures with cyclically monotone support and multivariate regu'] The aforementioned convergence induces a topology making $\mathrm{M}_{0}(\mathbb{R}^d)$ a Polish space with associated distance defined for all $\mu,\nu$ in $\mathrm{M}_{0}(\mathbb{R}^d)$ as 
\[
	d_0(\mu,\nu) = 
	\int_{0}^{\infty}
		e^{-r}
		p_r(\mu^{(r)},\nu^{(r)})
		[1+p_r(\mu^{(r)},\nu^{(r)})]^{-1}
	\mathrm{d}r
\]
where $p_r$ denotes the Prohorov metric and $m^{(r)}$ denotes the restriction of $m$ to $\mathbb{R}^d\setminus\mathds{B}_{0,r}$ for every $m\in\mathrm{M}_{0}(\mathbb{R}^d)$.

The topology induced by $d_0$ is the weakest topology that makes the evaluation maps $\mathrm{M}_{0}(\mathbb{R}^d) \to \left[0,\infty\right[ : \mu \mapsto \mu(f)$ continuous, where $f \in \mathrm{C}_{b,0}^{+}(\mathbb{R}^d)$.
A basis of this topology is formed by sets of the form 
\[
	\left\{
		\mu\in\mathrm{M}_{0}(\mathbb{R}^d) :  
		\mu(f_i)\in\left]a_i,b_i\right[, \,
		i=1,\ldots,K 
	\right\}
\]
for every $K\geqslant1$, $a_i<b_i,i\geqslant1$, $f_i\in\mathrm{C}_{b,0}^{+}(\mathbb{R}^d),i\geqslant1$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

