{"artifact_digest":"sha256:b2178f7dba572007a35bd3ac0e4533a80c4150fd47393c605e3eddcfd5613ebb","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/b2/b2178f7dba572007a35bd3ac0e4533a80c4150fd47393c605e3eddcfd5613ebb.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:38c1b7833ff34bd7d630ea5052ab723e03f2e228bec8d06d084de046565cfd25","bundle_entry_key":"2608-10415-theorem-281-theorem-281","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s1_053","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s1_053.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2608-10415-theorem-281-theorem-281","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: According to Theorem 10.19, the moments of the normal law $g_t$ must satisfy the following recurrence formula, with initial data $M_0=1$, $M_1=0$:
$$M_k=t(k-1)M_{k-2}$$

Now observe that by partial integration, we have the following formula:
\begin{eqnarray*}
\int_\mathbb Rx^ke^{-x^2/2t}dx
&=&\int_\mathbb R\frac{x^{k+1}}{k+1}\cdot\frac{x}{t}\cdot e^{-x^2/2t}dx\\
&=&\frac{1}{t(k+1)}\int_\mathbb Rx^{k+2}e^{-x^2/2t}dx
\end{eqnarray*}

Thus our integrals $I_k$ satisfy $I_{k+2}=t(k+1)I_k$, which is the same as $I_k=t(k-1)I_{k-2}$, same recurrence as for the numbers $M_k$. We also have $I_1=0$, because $e^{-x^2/2t}$ is an even function. So, it remains to suitably rescale our integrals, as to have ...","release_manifest_digest":"sha256:782c7ad25121c88ddaf75b66b3bbbff335ebb8e8c636819a79d48ef783146c21","revision_digest":"sha256:d1d3a39374933919934a31a4059b1007d06319cd571783e422117a4eb7e07a2d","source_id":"arxiv:2608.10415","source_revision":"tex-as-downloaded-2026-09-08","statement_fidelity_reviewed":false,"supersedes":[],"trust_class":"curated_secondary","truth_status":"unverified"}

