[Defi 1 of arXiv:2506.21087, 'Quasi-Stationary Distributions of Interacting Dynamical Systems and their approximation'] A probability $\nu\in{\cal P}(\MM)$ is called a Quasi-Stationary Distribution (QSD) for $(K_{\mu,\partial})_{\mu\in{\cal P}(\MM)}$ if $Y_0\sim\nu$ implies that
\begin{equation}
\forall n\in\mathbb{N},\quad \PE(Y_n\in .,\tau>n)=\nu(.)\PE(\tau>n).
\end{equation}

[Lemma lem:concentration of arXiv:2412.01381, 'Ergodicity and mixing for locally monotone stochastic evolution equations'] Assume that Hypotheses~(hyp:hemicont of the paper),~(hyp:coerc of the paper),~(hyp:coerc-g of the paper),~(hyp:monotonicity of the paper),~(hyp:monotonicity-g of the paper), (hyp:growth of the paper), and (hyp:regularity of the paper) are valid.
Then any invariant probability measure $\mu_\ast$ has finite $H$-moments of order $\alpha+\beta$. In addition,
\begin{equation}
\int_{H} \|z\|^{\alpha+\beta}_H\,\mu_\ast(\mathrm{d} z)\le\frac{2(c_1+c_2)}{c_0^\alpha\lambda_0\delta_1(\beta+2)}.
\end{equation}
In particular, 
\begin{equation}
\int_{H} \|z\|_H^2\,\mu_\ast(\mathrm{d} z)\le \left(\frac{2(c_1+c_2)}{c_0^\alpha\lambda_0\delta_1(\beta+2)}\right)^{2/(\alpha+\beta)}.
\end{equation}
Furthermore, $\mu_\ast$ has finite $V$-moments of order $\alpha$, and, in particular,
\begin{equation}
\int_{H} \|z\|^{\alpha}_V\,\mu_\ast(\mathrm{d} z)\le\frac{\|B\|^2_{L_2(U,H)}}{\delta_1}.
\end{equation}
Here, the constants are as in Hypotheses~(hyp:coerc of the paper),~(hyp:monotonicity of the paper),~(hyp:growth of the paper),~and~(hyp:quant of the paper), respectively.

