[Theorem 6 of arXiv:2506.22213, 'No arbitrage assumption implies the differentiability of derivative pricing function'] Let $\hat{X}$ be an $m$-dimensional continuous Markov semimartingale starting at $0$, then for $\hat{X}$, we have 
    \begin{align*}
        \hat{X}_t=A_t+M_t,
    \end{align*}
where $A$ is the finite variation part and $M$ is the local martingale with quadratic variation $N$. For such a process, there exists a system of local characteristics $(V,b,c)$ with $V$ is a strictly increasing continuous process, and $b,c$ are Borel measurable functions such that
\begin{align*}
    A_t=\int_0^tb(\hat{X}_s)dV_s,\quad\text{and}\quad N_t=\int_0^tc^2(\hat{X}_s)dV_s.
\end{align*}
Moreover, under some filtration $(\scG_t)_t$, we have an adapted stochastic process $X$ as a time-changed $\hat{X}$ such that
\begin{align*}
    X_t=\int_0^tb(X_s)ds+\int_0^tc(X_s)dW_s
\end{align*}
is an It\^{o} process.

