[Lemma eq:rhsPol of arXiv:2411.07157, 'Rough differential equations in the flow approach'] For $A_{{\eps,\mu}}$ given by the ansatz (equation eq:defFmu of the paper), we have
\begin{equs}
  {}& \text{$\mathrm{D}$} A_{{\eps,\mu}}\big[v,\dot G_\mu A_{{\eps,\mu}}[v]\big](t)=  
  \sum_{\substack{ \tau\in\text{\scriptsize{$\mathcal{T}_{*}$}} \\ (\tau_1,\tau_2)\in\text{\scriptsize{$\mathrm{Ind}(\tau)$}}}} \int_{\R^{\text{\tiny{$\mathfrak{s}$}}(\underline{\tau})}}
   \langle \big(\text{$\mathfrak{z}$}_{\eps,\mu}^{\tau_2} \,\graft^\mu\text{$\mathfrak{z}$}_{\eps,\mu}^{\tau_1}\big)^{\tau}(t,s^{\tau}),\Upsilon^{\tau}[v+{\tt u}](s^{\tau})\rangle_{\mathcal{H}^{\tau}}\mathrm{d} s^{\tau}\,.\textcolor{white}{blablablabl}
\end{equs}
In particular, the definition of $\Pi$ combined with (equation eq:rhsPol of the paper) implies that (equation eq:Pol of the paper) is equivalent to the following system of equations for the stationary force coefficients for $\tau \in \mathcal{T}$
\begin{equs}
   \d_\mu\text{$\mathfrak{z}$}_{\eps,\mu}^{\tau}(t,s^\tau)&=-\sum_{\substack{(\tau_1,\tau_2)\in\text{\scriptsize{$\mathrm{Ind}(\tau)$}}}}    \big(\text{$\mathfrak{z}$}_{\eps,\mu}^{\tau_2}   \,\graft^\mu\text{$\mathfrak{z}$}_{\eps,\mu}^{\tau_1}\big)^{\tau}(t,s^\tau)\,.
     \end{equs} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Prop 55 of arXiv:2510.23423, 'One-arm exponents of the high-dimensional Ising model'] Let $d>4$. There exists $c>0$ such that, for every $n\geq 1$,
\begin{equation}
	\mathbf P^{\emptyset,\emptyset}_{\beta_c}[0\connect{\n_1+\n_2\:}\partial \Lambda_n]\geq \frac{c}{n^{d-2}}.
\end{equation}

