[Lem lem-lazy-walk-flip of arXiv:2510.26123, 'Directed distances in bipolar-oriented triangulations:\\ exact exponents and scaling limit'] Let $\mathcal L$ be a random walk with i.i.d.\ increments which are uniform on $\{-1,0,1\}$. For $n\in\mathbbm N$, define
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\mathcal L}(n) := \mathcal L(n) - 2 \min_{ j \in [0,n]\cap\mathbbm Z } \mathcal L(j) .
\end{equation} 
In words, to obtain $\widehat{\mathcal L}$, every time $\mathcal L$ attains a running minimum (at which time it has a $-1$ step), we flip the $-1$ step to a $+1$ step. 
Then $\widehat{\mathcal L}$ is a random walk started from 0 and conditioned to stay non-negative in the sense of~[bd-conditioning] (see~(equation eqn-cond-walk-rn of the paper)).

