[Thm 52 (Backwards-time propagation of test functions in $d=2$) of arXiv:2510.22155, 'Random walks in space-time random media in all spatial dimensions: the full subcritical fl'] Let $d=2$. Assume $\x_N=(x^1_N,x^2_N)$ is any sequence such that $N^{-1/2}x_N^1 \to x_1$ and $x^1_N-x^2_N$ remains bounded, and consider the same setting as Assumption \ref{ass:SRI}. 
      Consider smooth bounded functions $\phi_1, ..., \phi_m : \mathbb R^d\to \mathbb R$ with globally bounded and continuous first and second partial derivatives, and consider $f^1,..., f^m : I\to \mathbb R$ of exponential decay at infinity. Then \begin{equation} 
    \lim_{N\to\infty} \Ebbnn \bigg[ \frac1{(\log N)^m}\sum_{0\leq r_1 < \ldots < r_m \leq Nt} \prod_{\ell =1}^m f^{\ell}(   R^1_{r_{\ell}}-   R^2_{r_{\ell}})\phi_{\ell}(N^{-1/2} (R^1_{r_\ell}-   s_N r_\ell))\bigg] = \prod_{\ell = 1}^m \bigg( \phi_\ell (x_1) \int_I f^\ell \; \dr \pi^{\mathrm{inv}} \bigg)\end{equation}
    The claim remains true if we replace $(f^1,...,f^m)$ on the left side by some sequence $(f^1_N,...,f^m_N)$ converging uniformly on compact sets as $N\to\infty$ to some $(f^1,...,f^m)$, as long as $\sup_{N} |f^j_N|$ decays exponentially fast at infinity for $j=1,...,m$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Corollary cor-homeomorphism of arXiv:2512.17727, 'Stochastic transport equation with L\'evy noise'] Under the assumptions of Theorem (d32 of the paper),   there exists an almost sure event $\Omega^{\prime\prime}\subset \bar{\Omega}^\prime$ such that,
for each $\omega \in \Omega^{\prime\prime}$ and $T>0$  there exists $C(\omega)>0$ (may dependent on $T$) such that  the following inequality holds
\begin{equation} 
\vert \phi_{s,t}^{-1}(y) -  y \vert \leq   \Vert  b \Vert_{0} \vert t- s\vert  + C(\omega), \;\; y \in \mathbb R^d, \; 0\le s \le t \le T.
\end{equation}
\begin{proof} It is enough to observe that since almost surely the trajectory $L: [0,T]\to \mathbb R^d$ is c\`adl\`ag,
there exists a $\mathbb{P}$-full event $\Omega^{\prime\prime} \subset \bar{\Omega}^\prime$ such that for $\omega\in \Omega^{\prime\prime}$,
$C(\omega):=\sup\{ \vert L_t(\omega) - L_s(\omega) \vert: 0\le s \le t \le T \}$  is finite.
\end{proof}

