    s_n^2 \to \infty \quad \text{as $n\to\infty$},
  \end{equation}
  and without changing the distribution of the process $\{S_n \colon n\ge 0\}$, we can redefine the process $\{S_n\colon n\ge 0\}$ on a richer probability space on which a standard Brownian motion $\{B(t)\colon t\ge 0\}$ is defined such that
\begin{equation}
  S_n -  B(s_n^2) = o(s_n^{1-\lambda}) \quad \text{as $n\to \infty$, a.s.}
\end{equation}
 for each $\lambda < \delta/16$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

