[Definition 22 of arXiv:2605.03851, 'The Relay Random Tree:\\A stochastic geometry approach of multihop relay in an urban visib'] \textbf{Reverse Shade the in finite range case.}

Let $\landsc$ be a landscape and $(a, b)\in \landsc$ a building. We define the reverse shade of $(a, b)$ as \textit{the intersection of all the shades of $(a, b)$ from buildings visible from $(a, b)$, cut off at distance $R$ from $a$}:
\begin{align*}
\rs (a, b) := \displaystyle\bigcap_{\substack{(a_1, b_1) \in \landsc \\ a_1\in ]a, a+R]}} \Biggl(\{(x, y) \in \Half: (x\geq a_1-R &\text{ and } \frac{b-y}{a-x} \geq \frac{b_1-b}{a_1-a}\}\\&  \cup\{(x, y)\in \Half:a-Rx <a_1-R\}\Biggl).
\end{align*}

\begin{figure}[h]
            \centering
            \includegraphics[width=0.75\textwidth]{Figures/reverse_shade_fini_horiz.ipe.png}
            \caption{The reverse shade is characterized by a succession of upwards jumps and then increasing slopes.}
\end{figure}

[Lemma lem:critical-window-mean-advantage of arXiv:2608.06159, 'Majority Dynamics on Resampled Sparse Erd\H{o}s--R\'enyi Graphs:\\[0.5ex] Gaussian Winner '] For every fixed \(C_0<\infty\), uniformly over deterministic initial
configurations satisfying (equation eq:critical-window-condition of the paper),
we have
\begin{align}
\frac{\E[\Delta_1\mid\rvy_0]}{\sqrt N}
=
x_0
+O\left(\frac1{\sqrt{\log N}}\right).

\end{align}
The implicit constant depends only on \(b\) and \(C_0\). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

