[Corollary InverseGammaU of arXiv:2504.18739, 'discrete analogues of second-order Riesz transforms'] Let $S$ be a random variable with an inverse Gamma distribution of parameters $\alpha$ and $\beta$ given by $\alpha=\frac{d+2}{2}$, $\beta=\frac{|m|^2}{4}$. Let $B_t$ be the standard $d$-dimensional Brownian motion. Then, for $m\in\mathbb{Z}^d$, with $m\ne 0$,
\begin{align}
    U(m)=-\mathbb{E}\left(\frac{1}{H_{S}\left(\frac{B_S}{\sqrt{2}}+\frac{m}{2}\right)}\right) \quad \text{and}\quad U(m)=U(-m). 
\end{align}

