[Definition def:PI of arXiv:2509.04155, 'An Approach to Sub-Gaussian Heat Kernel Estimates via Analysis on Metric Spaces'] Fix $p \geq 1$ and let $\mathcal{F}_p$ and $\Gamma_p$ be as in (1)-(2) of Definition (def:SSS of the paper).
    For a given scale function $\Psi$, we say that the pair  $(\mathcal{F}_p,\Gamma_p)$ satisfies the \emph{Poincar\'e inequality} (eq:PI of the paper) if there are constants $C,\sigma \geq 1$ satisfying the following condition.
    For all $x \in X$, $r > 0$ and $f \in \mathcal{F}_p$,
    \begin{equation}
        \tag{$\textup{PI}_p(\Psi)$}
        \int_{B(x,r)} \abs{f - f_{B(x,r)}}^p \, d\mu \leq C \Psi(x,r) \int_{B(x,\sigma r)} \, d\Gamma_p\Span{f}.
    \end{equation}

[Lemma 20 (Boundary term) of arXiv:2604.12797, 'A free boundary problem for the mean-field limit of diffusing particles with nonlinear bou'] We have
	\begin{equation*}
	\lim_{\varepsilon \downarrow 0} \expect{ \int_0^t \int_0^{\infty} p_{\varepsilon}(x) F^{\Delta, \varepsilon}_s(x)  \dx \di  \big( I^{\mu}_s - I_s^{\nu} \big)}
	= - \frac{1}{4} \expect{ \big( I^{\mu}_t - I_t^{\nu}  \big)^2   }.
	\end{equation*}

