[Theorem thm:uncountable many mixing measure of arXiv:2411.03512, 'Ergodicity and Mixing of Sublinear Expectation System and Applications'] The $G$-Brownian motion $B_t(\omega)=\omega_t$ is a martingale on $(\Omega,\mathcal{F},(\mathcal{F}_t)_{t\geq 0}, P_{\eta})$ for each $\eta\in \mathcal{A}_{0,\infty}^{Q}$ and
		\begin{equation}
			\mathbb{E}_{P_{\eta}}\Big[\phi\big(\langle B,B\rangle_t^{P_{\eta}}\big)\Big]={\mathbb{E}_{\tilde P}}\bigg[\phi\bigg(\int_0^t\eta_sds\bigg)\bigg], \ \text{ for all bounded measurable } \ \phi:\mathbb R^d\to \mathbb R,
		\end{equation}
        where $\langle B,B\rangle^{P_{\eta}}$ is the quadratic variance process of $B$ under $P_{\eta}$.
		Moreover, for any $q\in Q$, $\{B_t\}_{t\geq 0}$ is a Brownian motion with covariance $(qt)_{t\geq 0}$ under $P_{q}$ and $(\Omega,\mathcal{F}, (\theta_t)_{t\geq 0}, P_{q})$ is strongly mixing.

