[Lemma 22 of arXiv:2605.30055, 'The Wasserstein cost of importance sampling'] For $n$ large enough, we have
\begin{equation}
 n^{p/d-1}\EE\sqa{\Wb_{p,Q}^p(\gamma\nu_n,\lambda f)}\geqslant (1-\eps)(1-o_{L}(1))  \bD \int_{Q} g f^{-p/d} -C_\eps o_L(1) |Q|,
\end{equation}
where $\lim_{L\to \infty } o_L(1)=0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma mean of Phi of arXiv:2605.20800, 'On the maximal displacement of subcritical branching random walk in random environment'] Given $\xi$ and $\overline{Y}$, any $k\leqslant -1$, under $P_{\xi,\overline{Y}}$, the distribution of $\Phi(\mathcal{T}_k,\overline{Y},0)$ depends only on the pair $(\overline{Y}_k,\Gamma_k(\xi))$, where $\Gamma_k$ is defined in (cut off operator of the paper), and for different $k$, these random variables are independent with each other.
		And if the pair $ (\overline{Y}_k,\Gamma_k(\xi)) $ is equal to the pair $ (H_{\omega_k}-x,\Gamma_k(\xi)) $ in (Lemma of spine decomposition of the paper), then these two random variables has the same conditional distribution.
		Moreover, we have
		\begin{equation}
			P_{\xi,\overline{Y}} \left[\Phi(\mathcal{T}_k,\overline{Y}_k,0)\right] = \eta_{k+1}.
		\end{equation}

