for some $0 < \theta < t$, so in particular the left hand side is positive and $O(t^{k+1})$ as $t \to 0$. Therefore, $C_p > 0$ is well-defined, i.e. the integral defining $C_p$ converges (it converges if and only if $k+1 - p - 1 > -1$ and $-p-1+k < -1$, that is $k < p < k+1$). For a fixed $x > 0$, the change of variables $t \mapsto tx$ in the integral defining $C_p$ yields the formula for","release_manifest_digest":"sha256:958d70c97929155a8138fba14430ff53da68bbdbffce9b68c883408b9aec1dd1","revision_digest":"sha256:e6526e7d4aa33642b5f4c69093820241562d3dafb072b3af0b4ccf1fdc497327","source_id":"arxiv:2602.03058","source_revision":"tex-as-downloaded-2026-09-08","statement_fidelity_reviewed":false,"supersedes":[],"trust_class":"curated_secondary","truth_status":"unverified"}

