[Theorem T:Nachmias_diam of arXiv:2512.21977, 'Repeat times and a two-weight UST model'] Fix $\varepsilon > 0$ and $\lambda \in \mathbb{R}$. There exists $A = A(\varepsilon, \lambda) < \infty$ and $n_0=n_0(\varepsilon,\lambda)\in \mathbb{N}$ such that
\begin{equation}
    \mathbb{P}_{n,p} \big( \exists \text{connected component } \mathcal{C} \text{ with } \operatorname{diam}(\mathcal{C}) > An^{1/3} \big) < \varepsilon
\end{equation}
for all $n\geq n_0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma 3 (Classification of all fixed points) of arXiv:2608.14802, 'Dynamical Gibbs-non-Gibbs transitions for finite-alphabet models on trees'] Assume that $\theta>1+2\sqrt{2}$ and define 
\begin{equation*}
    x_\pm:=
\left(
\frac{\theta-1\pm\sqrt{(\theta-1)^2-8}}{2}
\right)^2.
\end{equation*}
Furthermore, for each $i\in \{1,2,3\}$, define the vectors $l^{(i)}_-,l^{(i)}_+\in \Delta^2$ componentwise by
\begin{equation}
    l^{(i)}_\pm(j):=\frac{1+(x_\pm-1)\delta_{ij}}{x_\pm+2}
\end{equation}
for $j\in \{1,2,3\}$.
Then, for $\theta\neq 4$, the points $l^{(i)}_-$ where $i\in \{1,2,3\}$ are precisely the fixed points of $f$ with exactly one unstable direction, that is, the fixed points $l$ for which $Df(l)$ has exactly one eigenvalue of modulus strictly greater than $1$. We refer to these fixed points as $f$-saddles. The remaining symmetry-breaking fixed points are $l^{(i)}_+$ where $i\in \{1,2,3\}$, and they are all $f$-stable.
Moreover, both eigenvalues of $Df$ at the free fixed point $l_{\text{free}}:=\left(\frac{1}{3},\frac{1}{3},\frac{1}{3}\right)$ have modulus strictly greater than $1$ if $\theta>4$ and strictly smaller than $1$ if $\theta<4$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma 24 of arXiv:2510.24244, 'Statistical properties of Markov shifts: part II-LLT'] For every $0<\delta<T$ and $L\in\bbN$ there exists $0<\delta(L)<1/2$ such that if
\begin{equation}

\varepsilon\leq \delta(L) 
\end{equation}
then  for every $j$ and $\ell\leq L+1$ we have the following.
If  for some nonzero real $t$ such that $\del \leq |t|\leq T$ there exists $h$ with 
$\|h\|_{\om,*}\leq 1$ and
\begin{equation}

\inf_{x}|\cL_{j,t}^{\ell}h(x)|\geq 1-\varepsilon 
\end{equation}
then
\begin{equation}
f_{j+\ell-1}=g_{j,t,\ell}+H_{j+\ell-1}-H_{j+\ell}\circ T_{j+\ell-1}+(2\pi/t) Z_{t,j,\ell}
\end{equation}
where $Z_{t,j,\ell}$ is integer valued, and for every $0<r<1$ we have 
$\sup_{t,j,\ell}\|g_{t,j,\ell}\|_{j+\ell,\om^r}\leq c_r(\ell)$ where $c_r(\ell)\to 0$ as $\ell\to\infty$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition 2 of arXiv:2609.02755, 'The Sharp Rate of Probabilistically Strong Convergence to the KPZ Equation'] Fix $0<\kappa \le \kappa_0$ for $\kappa_0$ sufficiently\footnote{For example, $\kappa_0 = 1/100$ would do} small. We write $\mfT$ for the regularity structure generated by the noise types $\mathfrak{L}_- = \{\<b0>, \<p0>, \<0t>\}$ and kernel types $\mfL_+ = \{\<k>, \<bk>, \<pk>\}$ where the rule (in the sense of \cite[Definition 5.7]{BHZ}) is the one naturally associated to the system \eqref{eq:main_sys}. In particular, each $\mathcal{I}_{\diamond}$ for $\diamond \in \mfL_+$ is a degree $2$ integration operator and
\begin{equ}
	|\<b0>|_\fs = |\<p0>|_\fs = - 3/2 - \kappa, \qquad |\<0t>|_\fs = - 2- \kappa.
\end{equ}
Further, we write $\mcT$ for the canonical basis of the model space of $\mfT$, which is therefore henceforth denoted by $\scal{\mcT}$.

[Proposition L-G Trans of arXiv:2605.12777, 'Mesoscopic Rates of Convergence for Complex Wishart Matrices at the Leftmost Spectrum Edge'] Given the differential equation (equation W-equa1tr of the paper), let $I_1=(a_1,b_1)$, $I_2=(a_2,b_2)$ be two intervals containing exactly one turning point of $f$, say, $z_1\in I_1$ and $z_2\in I_2$. Define $\zeta$ by
\begin{equation}
\left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
  \frac{2}{3}\zeta^{\frac{3}{2}}(z)= \int_{z}^{z_1}f^{\frac{1}{2}}(t)dt,~z\le z_1\\\\
  \frac{2}{3}(-\zeta)^\frac{3}{2}(z)=\int_{z_1}^z(-f)^\frac{1}{2}(t)dt,~z>z_1
\end{array}
 \right. 
\end{equation}
on $I_1$, and similarly,
\begin{equation}
\left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
  \frac{2}{3}\zeta^{\frac{3}{2}}(z)= \int_{z_2}^{z}f^{\frac{1}{2}}(t)dt,~z>z_2\\\\
  \frac{2}{3}(-\zeta)^\frac{3}{2}(z)=\int_{z}^{z_2}(-f)^\frac{1}{2}(t)dt,~z\le z_2
\end{array}
 \right. 
\end{equation}
on $I_2$. Then for any $z\in I_j$ where $j\in\{1,2\}$, $\zeta':=\frac{d\zeta}{dz}$ is positive when $z>z_j$ and negative when $z<z_j$. Moreover, for any $z\in I_j$, define $W:=(\zeta')^{\frac{1}{2}}w$ when $z>z_j$ and $W:=(-\zeta')^\frac{1}{2}w$ when $z\le z_j$. Further define $\tilde{f}:=\frac{f}{\zeta}$, which equals $(\zeta')^2$. Then the previous equation (equation W-equa1tr of the paper) can be expressed as:
\begin{equation}
    \frac{d^2W(\zeta)}{d\zeta^2}=\left(\kappa_N^2\zeta+\Psi(\zeta)\right)W(\zeta)
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation*}
  \Psi(\zeta)=\tilde{f}^{-\frac{1}{4}}\frac{d^2}{d\zeta^2}\left(\tilde{f}^{\frac{1}{4}}\right)+\frac{g}{\tilde{f}}.
\end{equation*}
This $\zeta$ is called the \textbf{Liouville--Green~transformation} of $f$.

