[Lemma 14 of arXiv:2506.08910, 'Critical points of random polynomials and finite free cumulants'] Let $k\in\N$. Then, in distribution  \begin{equation}
			\lim\limits_{N\rightarrow\infty} N^{1-k}\kappa_{k}^{N}(\tilde{q}_N)=\lim\limits_{N\rightarrow\infty}Nm_{k}({q}_N)=\begin{cases}
				Y,\ k=1,\\
				\sigma^2+\sum_{j\in\Z_{\neq0}}\alpha_{j}^2,\ k=2,\\
				\sum_{j\in\Z_{\neq0}}\alpha_{j}^{k},\ k>2,
			\end{cases}
	\end{equation} where  $\{\alpha_{j}\}_{j\in\Z_{\neq0}}$ is a Poisson point process with intensity measure $\nu$ and $Y$ is as defined in \eqref{eq:point process Y def}. Moreover, this convergence is joint for any finite collection of $k_1,k_2,\dots,k_{m}\in\N$.

