{"artifact_digest":"sha256:f011056a12ac27008785da5daf9fa046a34849f2258f27d3996281f10bf1958a","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/f0/f011056a12ac27008785da5daf9fa046a34849f2258f27d3996281f10bf1958a.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:31edeaa169f95b92894843f0d9a96b4802897feee5d66587efe0dec110ffeed4","bundle_entry_key":"2604-21663-lemma-30-measurable-density","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s1_017","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s1_017.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2604-21663-lem-30-measurable-density","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: By the Radon-Nikodym Theorem, for all $x\in \mathbb R^d$, the measure $p(x,\cdot)$ has a density, which we denote $g(x,\cdot)$. The measurability of $g$ as a function of $\mathbb R^d \times \mathbb R^d$ may fail if functions $g(x,\cdot)$ are not precisely chosen.
	Let $\mathcal D_n$ denote a countable partition of $\mathbb R^d$ into cubes of side $2^{-n}$, 
	and set 
	\begin{equation*}
		f_n(x,y):=2^{dn}\sum_{C\in \mathcal D_n}\mathbf 1_C(y)p(x,C)
		,\qquad x,y\in \mathbb R^d.
	\end{equation*}
	Since it is a countable sum of measurable functions, $f_n$ is measurable. 
	Hence, the pointwise limit superior of $f_n$ is also measurable. We denote it $f$ and we  show that it is a density of $p$. ...","release_manifest_digest":"sha256:1886ee78d31e59a283e07db24a4a31c9e7f808d0466ab5a897e9416c296038c8","revision_digest":"sha256:eb8c71cf9939f2ad4461beb0f81f828404ddf7406afca41582e80f928c971129","source_id":"arxiv:2604.21663","source_revision":"tex-as-downloaded-2026-09-07","statement_fidelity_reviewed":false,"supersedes":[],"trust_class":"curated_secondary","truth_status":"unverified"}

