[Lemma lem:whitenoiseregularity of arXiv:2607.24640, 'The Yang-Mills measure on surfaces via Morse theory'] For every $\psi\in L^\infty(\Sigma, \mathbb{R} )$, the random series
\begin{equation}
F_\psi^N:=\sum_{n= 1}^N\sum_{\ell=1}^L  X_{n,\ell} \psi e_n \mathfrak{b}_\ell    
\end{equation}
converges almost surely to some limit $F_\psi$ in $L^2(\Omega,H^{-1-\kappa}(\Sigma,\mathfrak{g}))$ for all $\kappa>0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition 16 of arXiv:2606.23433, 'Emergence of Common Noise: Quantitative Conditional Propagation of Chaos'] Let $X=(X^{1},\ldots ,X^{N})$ and $\bar{X}=(\bar{X}
^{1},\ldots ,\bar{X}^{N})\in L_{s}^{2}({\mathbb{R}}^{d\times N}).$ Then
almost surely, 
\begin{equation*}
\Vert \sqrt{F_{N}(X)}-\sqrt{F_{N}(\bar{X})}\Vert _{op}\leq \frac{8(\log
d_{0}+2)^{2}}{\sqrt{f_{\ast }}}\Vert F_{N}(X)-F_{N}(\bar{X})\Vert _{op}.
\end{equation*}
In particular, recalling that $\Vert A\Vert _{op}^{2}\leq \Vert A\Vert
_{HS}^{2}\leq d_{0}\Vert A\Vert _{op}^{2},$ and using the Lipschitz
continuity of $f,$ 
\begin{eqnarray}
\Vert \overline{S}_{s,t}^{N}(X,f)-\overline{S}_{s,t}^{N}(\bar{X},f)\Vert
_{2}^{2} &\leq &\frac{C_{d_{0}}}{f_{\ast }}L_{f}^{2}(t-s)E|X-\bar{X}
|_{N}^{2},   \\
with\quad C_{d_{0}} &=&8^{2}d_{0}(\log d_{0}+2)^{4}.
\end{eqnarray}
As a consequence, the Lipschitz property (\ref{Aiii}) holds with $\overline{L
}=\frac{C_{d_{0}}}{f_{\ast }}L_{f}^{2}.$

