[Theorem thm:main_cycle of arXiv:2603.28936, 'Composition of random functions and word reconstruction'] Let $\pi$ be a primitive word and let $w=\pi^d$ for some $d\geq 1$.
For any fixed integer $L\geq 1$,  we have

\begin{align}
	(C^\pi_{1,n},C^\pi_{2,n}, \dots, C^\pi_{L,n})
	\stackrel{(d)}{\longrightarrow}
	(Z_1,Z_2,\dots,Z_L),
\end{align}
	where $Z_1,Z_2,\dots$ are independent random variables with $Z_\ell \sim \text{Po}(1/\ell)$ for $\ell \in \mathbb{N}$. 
Moreover, 
\begin{align}
(C^w_{1,n}, C^w_{2,n}, \dots, C^w_{L,n})
	\stackrel{(d)}{\longrightarrow}
	\Psi(Z_1,Z_2,\dots,Z_{dL})
\end{align}
	where $\Psi(x_1,x_2,\dots,x_{dL})=(y_1,y_2,\dots,y_L)$ with
	$y_i:= \sum_{c\in[d]:c|d\atop \gcd{i}{c}=1} (d/c) x_{di/c}.$

	In both cases, the convergence also holds in the sense of  moments. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma lem:milne-factorization-comp of arXiv:2609.00698, 'Kinetic Wedge Layers and Diffusive Limit of Neutron Transport in Polygonal Domains'] For every $0<\kappa<\kappa_1<1$ and every $H\in Y_{\kappa_1}$, there is a unique pair $(E,\rho_{\mathrm{dec}})\in\mathbb R\times Y_\kappa$ such that $(I-K_+)(E+\rho_{\mathrm{dec}})=H$ on $\mathbb R_+$. Moreover the map $H\mapsto(E,\rho_{\mathrm{dec}})$ is linear and
\begin{align}
 \abs E+\nm{\rho_{\mathrm{dec}}}_{Y_\kappa}
 \le C_{\kappa,\kappa_1}\nm H_{Y_{\kappa_1}}.

\end{align}
Uniqueness is asserted only in the class $\mathbb R\oplus Y_\kappa$. Nothing is claimed about arbitrary distributional solutions of the half-line equation.

[Lemma 14 of arXiv:2608.02927, 'Thinning Operation via the Poisson-F\"ollmer Process'] Let $X$ be a random variable with density $f$ with respect to $\pi_{T}.$ Assume that $f$ is bounded away from zero and infinity and $H(X|\pi_{T}) < \infty.$ Then, for every $a$ in $[0,1],$ we have 
    \begin{displaymath}
        \frac{d}{da} \big(  H  ( T_{a}(X) | \pi_{aT} ) \big) = T \E \phi( \lambda_{aT} ).
    \end{displaymath}
    Moreover, $a \mapsto H\big( T_{a}(X) | \pi_{aT}  \big)$ is convex.

