[Theorem 9 (Universal covariance envelope for fixed-pool volume-sampled least squares) of arXiv:2608.26877, 'When Is the Sharp Covariance Envelope Tight? Feature-Only Geometry for Volume-Sampled Leas'] For every full-column-rank $X$, every fixed $y$, $m>d$, and
$d\le s\le m$,
\begin{equation}
  \E_X w_S=w^*.

\end{equation}
Consequently $M_s:=C_s$ is the covariance of $w_S$, and
\begin{equation}
  M_s\preceq \alpha\lossstar G^{-1},
  \qquad
  \overline M_s:=G^{1/2}M_sG^{1/2}
  \preceq \alpha\lossstar I_d.

\end{equation}
Moreover,
\begin{equation}
  \E_X\lVert Xw_S-y\rVert_2^2
  \le (1+d\alpha)\lossstar
  =\left(1+\frac{d(m-s)}{m-d}\right)\lossstar.

\end{equation}
The coefficient in~\eqref{eq:ue-envelope} is attained by the family in
Proposition~\ref{prop:ue-attainment}.  For positive-loss row-general-position
designs with $d\ge2$ it is strictly unattained when $d<s<m$, but it remains
their operator-norm supremum as described in
Proposition~\ref{prop:ue-interior-supremum}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

