{"artifact_digest":"sha256:3575c32133b85113c72092c7d0aaf5089aeeda24d8a6ae13428b89cc081ba9a7","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/35/3575c32133b85113c72092c7d0aaf5089aeeda24d8a6ae13428b89cc081ba9a7.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:bf93c60c30f4413657bb81b35565e28b8ad1a191c7e63d8340d5c3bfb1f7a5d1","bundle_entry_key":"2511-00764-theorem-15-pr-250930","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s1_003","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s1_003.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2511-00764-proposition-15-pr-250930","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: The proof is similar to that of Proposition 6 in \cite{CHWZ25}. First, note that if there exists at least one $X_j>c$ with $j\in [n]$, then $\sum^n_{i=1} \eta_i (X_i\wedge c)\ge \eta_j X_j\ge \eta_{(1)} c=c/b$. Thus, for $x\in [0, c/b)$, we have
\begin{align*}
  \P\(\sum^n_{i=1} \eta_i Y_i \le x\) & = \P\(\sum^n_{i=1} \eta_i Y_i \le x,\ X_1\le c, \ldots, X_n\le c\) \\
  & = \P\(\sum^n_{i=1} \eta_i X_i \le x,\ X_1\le c, \ldots, X_n\le c\) \\
  & = \P\(\sum^n_{i=1} \eta_i X_i \le x\).
\end{align*}
