[Cor 9 of arXiv:2606.21778, 'Pontryagin Maximum Principle in Free Probability Theory'] Let $0<T<\infty$. Consider the fSDE
	\begin{equation}
		dX_t=a(X_t)dt + \sum_{i=1}^{d}b^i(X_t)dW_tc^i(X_t)
	\end{equation}
	Assume that $a$, and the products $b^ic^i, i=1,\dots d$ are globally operator Lipschitz. Then \eqref{eq:h54} has a unique global solution on $[0,T]$, uniformly bounded in operator norm.

[Proposition 4 (Posterior expectations of the complete-data sufficient statistics) of arXiv:2608.21551, '\bf Sparse Separable Factor Analysis in the Complex Domain with an Application to Local Fi'] At iteration $t$, define
\begin{align*}
  \widetilde{\Sigmab}^{(t)}
  =
  \tilde{\Lambdab}^{(t)}
  \Gammab^{(t)}
  \tilde{\Lambdab}^{(t)\star}
  +
  \Psib^{(t)},\quad
  \widetilde{\Ab}^{(t)}
  =
  \Gammab^{(t)}
  \tilde{\Lambdab}^{(t)\star}
  \left(\widetilde{\Sigmab}^{(t)}\right)^{-1},\quad
  \widetilde{\Cb}^{(t)}
  =
  \Gammab^{(t)}
  -
  \widetilde{\Ab}^{(t)}
  \tilde{\Lambdab}^{(t)}
  \Gammab^{(t)}.
\end{align*}
Then, the conditional distribution of the latent factor matrix is
\begin{align*}
  \tilde{\Zb}\mid\Xb,\tilde{\thetab}^{(t)}
  \sim
  \mathbb{C}\Ncal_{k\times n}
  \left(
    \widetilde{\Ab}^{(t)}\Xb,
    \widetilde{\Cb}^{(t)},
    \Ib_n
  \right),
\end{align*}
and the posterior expectations of the complete-data sufficient statistics in \eqref{eq:sufficient_stat_defs} are
\begin{align}

  \tilde{\Zb}^{(t)}
  =
  \widetilde{\Ab}^{(t)}\Xb, \qquad
  \widetilde{\Sbb}_{zz}^{(t)}
  =
  n\widetilde{\Cb}^{(t)}
  +
  \tilde{\Zb}^{(t)}
  \tilde{\Zb}^{(t)\star}.
\end{align}

[Definition 68 of arXiv:2604.01685, 'Probability with Measure ootnote{Lecture notes. University of Ljubljana.}'] The $f$ from the preceding theorem is denoted $ rac{\dd \mu}{\dd
u}$ and called the Radon-Nikodym derivative (also, density) of $\mu$ w.r.t. $
u$. Statements involving it are asserted for any of its versions (as a result they tend to attract the a.e. qualifier). ootnote{Alternatively, to circumvent the issue of the derivatives being defined only a.e. precisely, one could pass (passes) to their equivalence classes w.r.t. a.e. equality, but it seems an even bigger nuissance than having to live with a.e. qualifiers.}
		  Sometimes we write $ rac{\mu(\dd x)}{
u(\dd x)}$ when we wish to provide an expression for $ rac{\dd\mu}{\dd 
u}$ at the point $x$.

[Definition 2 of arXiv:2605.04684, 'Ergodicity of stochastic functional differential equation with jumps and finite delay'] A distance-like function $d$ bounded by $1$ is called contracting for $P_{t}$ if there exists $\alpha<1$ such that for any $\xi,\eta \in \mathscr{D}$ with $ d(\xi,\eta)<1$, we have
\begin{equation*}
    W_{d}\left(P_{t}(\xi, \cdot), P_{t}(\eta, \cdot)\right) \leq \alpha d(\xi, \eta),
\end{equation*}
where $P_t(\xi,\cdot)$ and $P_t(\eta,\cdot)$ are the transition functions of the process $(X_{t}^{\xi})_{t \geq 0}$ and $(X_{t}^{\eta})_{t \geq 0}$, respectively.

[Lemma Hlfsn of arXiv:2609.01990, 'Jacquet-Zagier treatment of the beyond endoscopy trace formula for \(\GL_2\)'] We can write \[
    H_{l,f}^s(0,n) = J_{l,f}(0,n) + \left(\frac{\sqrt{\pi} lf^2}{2 \sqrt{n}}\right)^{2s-1} I_{l,f}(0,n) 
    \] as 
    \begin{equation}
        H_{l,f}^s(0,n) = \frac{1}{2\pi i } \int_{(2)} \frac{(2w - \frac{3}{2})}{(w+\frac{s}{2}-1)(w-\frac{s+1}{2}) } \Gamma(w) \mathcal{F}(w,s) \left(\frac{\sqrt{\pi}lf^2}{2\sqrt{n}} \right)^{-2w+s+1} dw 
    \end{equation}
    with 
    \begin{equation}
        \mathcal{F}(w,s) = \int_{-1}^{1} F_s(y) (1-y^2)^{w - \frac{s+1}{2}} dy 
    \end{equation}

