[Lemma 5 of arXiv:2608.01081, 'The Optimal Rate in the Averaged Random-Marginal Central Limit Theorem for Log-Concave Mea'] Let $n\ge2$, let $G=(G_1,\ldots,G_n)$ have independent standard Gaussian
coordinates, and put $U=G/|G|$. Then $U$ is uniform on $S^{n-1}$ and is
independent of $|G|$. If $k_1,\ldots,k_r$ are nonnegative integers and
$K=k_1+\cdots+k_r$, then
\begin{equation}
 \E\prod_{j=1}^rU_j^{2k_j}
 =\frac{\prod_{j=1}^r(2k_j-1)!!}
 {n(n+2)\cdots(n+2K-2)}.
\end{equation}
Every moment containing an odd power of one coordinate is zero. Also,
$U_1$ has density
\begin{equation}
 c_n(1-u^2)^{(n-3)/2}\mathbf1_{[-1,1]}(u),
 \qquad
 c_n=\frac{\Gamma(n/2)}{\sqrt\pi\,\Gamma((n-1)/2)}.
\end{equation}

